<%
  /*
   * WORKER-ONLY VIEW. The Express app in src/ has no /admin/backup page — its
   * GET /admin/backup.zip streams a real zip straight off the disk (see
   * src/services/backup.js), and views/admin/dashboard.ejs still points there
   * on that stack through its typeof-guarded backupHref local. This file is
   * rendered by worker/src/routes/admin.js and nothing else; it lives in the
   * shared views/ directory because that is where the compiler looks, and it
   * is compared engine-to-engine by worker/build/view-parity.mjs like every
   * other template.
   */
%>
<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">Leaders only</p>
    <h1>Backups</h1>
    <p class="lede measure">
      Everything this board holds — members, posts, meetings, drafts, comments
      and the uploaded files themselves — lives in one managed database. There
      is no disk to copy and nothing this site quietly backs up somewhere else
      on your behalf. <strong>The download on this page is the backup.</strong>
      Grab one now and then, and keep it somewhere that isn't Cloudflare.
    </p>

    <div class="card <%= backupStale ? 'card--warn' : '' %>">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--muted">Your last download</p>
      <p>
        <% if (lastBackupAt) { %>
          <span class="badge <%= backupStale ? 'badge--warn' : 'badge--ok' %>"><%= dates.relative(lastBackupAt) %></span>
        <% } else { %>
          <span class="badge badge--warn">Never downloaded</span>
        <% } %>
      </p>
      <p class="form-actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/admin/backup/download.sql">Download backup (.sql)</a>
      </p>
      <p class="hint hint--block">
        A plain-text SQL file with every member, post, announcement, meeting,
        draft record and comment as <span class="mono">INSERT</span>
        statements — open it in any text editor, or load it into a fresh
        database with the <span class="mono">sqlite3</span> command line.
        Downloading refreshes the "last backed up" badge on the
        <a href="/admin">admin dashboard</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--muted">The files themselves</p>
      <% if (!fileCount) { %>
        <p class="measure">
          Nothing is stored yet — no drafts, event attachments or chat
          attachments. The download above is a complete backup as it stands.
        </p>
      <% } else if (filesInline) { %>
        <p class="measure">
          <span class="badge badge--ok"><%= fileCount %> file<%= fileCount === 1 ? '' : 's' %></span>
          <span class="mono small muted"><%= Math.max(1, Math.round(fileBytes / 1024)) %> KB</span>
          — small enough to travel inside the download above, so that one file
          really is everything. Drafts, event attachments and chat attachments
          are all in there, bytes and all.
        </p>
      <% } else { %>
        <p class="measure">
          <span class="badge badge--warn"><%= fileCount %> file<%= fileCount === 1 ? '' : 's' %></span>
          <span class="mono small muted"><%= Math.round(fileBytes / 1024 / 1024 * 10) / 10 %> MB</span>
          — too much to fit in one download. Converting file bytes into text
          costs this site a fixed amount of work per megabyte, and a single
          request only gets a few milliseconds of it, so the files come down in
          numbered parts instead. The main download above has everything else,
          plus a list of exactly what is in each part.
        </p>
        <ul class="file-list">
          <% fileParts.forEach(function (p) { %>
            <li>
              <a href="/admin/backup/files.sql?part=<%= p.part %>">Part <%= p.part %> of <%= fileParts.length %></a>
              <span class="mono small muted">
                <%= Math.max(1, Math.round(p.bytes / 1024)) %> KB
              </span>
              <% if (p.oversize) { %>
                <span class="badge badge--warn">may time out</span>
              <% } %>
            </li>
          <% }) %>
        </ul>
        <p class="hint hint--block">
          Save every part alongside the main download. A restore needs all of
          them.
        </p>
        <% if (fileParts.some(function (p) { return p.oversize; })) { %>
          <p class="hint hint--block">
            The parts marked <strong>may time out</strong> each hold one piece
            of a file bigger than this site can turn into text in the few
            milliseconds a single request is allowed. Try them — they often
            work — but if one fails, the whole board (files included) can be
            exported from a terminal by whoever holds the Cloudflare account,
            with no such limit:
          </p>
          <pre class="mono small" style="overflow-x: auto;">npx wrangler d1 export afwc-board --remote --output afwc-full.sql</pre>
          <p class="hint hint--block">
            That single file is a complete backup on its own — nothing else on
            this page is needed alongside it.
          </p>
        <% } %>
      <% } %>
      <p class="hint hint--block">
        Files are kept for <strong><%= retentionDays %> days</strong> and then
        cleaned up automatically — change that on the
        <a href="/admin">dashboard</a>. Anything already cleaned up is gone
        from the backups too, which is the other good reason to take one now
        and then.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--muted">To put it back</p>
      <p class="measure">
        Restoring needs a terminal, not a browser button — a one-time job for
        whoever holds the group's Cloudflare account, and the reason the
        repository's README carries the long version:
      </p>
      <pre class="mono small" style="overflow-x: auto;"># A local copy you can open and read:
cat worker/migrations/*.sql afwc-backup-*.sql | sqlite3 restored.db

# Back into the live board (careful — this writes to production):
wrangler d1 execute afwc-board --remote --file afwc-backup-2026-08-08.sql</pre>
      <p class="hint hint--block">
        The migrations create the empty schema; the backup file fills it. Load
        the main file first, then any file parts, in any order.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--muted">The monthly check</p>
      <p>
        <% if (lastSnapshotAt) { %>
          On the 1st of each month this board checks itself over — how much it
          is storing, whether anything looks off — and records the result.
          Most recent: <span class="badge badge--ok"><%= dates.relative(lastSnapshotAt) %></span>
          (<%= lastSnapshotMonth %>).
        <% } else { %>
          <span class="badge badge--warn">Not run yet</span> — this happens on
          its own on the 1st of the month; there's nothing to click for it.
        <% } %>
      </p>
      <p class="hint hint--block">
        It does <strong>not</strong> make a copy of anything. Nothing does,
        except you, on this page. It exists so the numbers here stay fresh and
        so a problem gets noticed by something other than a person going
        looking for one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p><a class="btn btn--sm" href="/admin">Back to the dashboard</a></p>
  </div>
</section>
